# Student A – Initial Design

## Problem
Design a method to choose between Merge Sort and Quick Sort based on the requirements of the input data.

## Proposed Approach
My approach is to select the sorting algorithm based on stability, memory usage, and worst-case performance.

1. If stable sorting is required, choose Merge Sort.
2. If guaranteed O(n log n) time is required, choose Merge Sort.
3. If extra memory should be minimized, choose Quick Sort.
4. Otherwise, choose Quick Sort for its good average-case performance.

## Reason
Merge Sort provides stable sorting and guaranteed O(n log n) time, but it requires extra memory.

Quick Sort is generally fast in practice and can sort in-place, but its worst-case time is O(n²).

## Conclusion
The algorithm should be selected according to the user's requirements rather than choosing one sorting method for every situation.
